\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter plot: death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). Each point is one of 11 ScienceWorld task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in agent death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$. The resulting Death Avoidance Factor (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage over internal reasoning is attributable to death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

This concentration is consistent across task types. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}. Knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap for a given task type. The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes compared to 16.3\% for deterministic computation---a 2.6$\times$ ratio that drives the bulk of the observed score difference.

The episode-level label distribution reflects this asymmetry: of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}---when internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals the residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths under both strategies yet shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences---deterministic computation can provide advantages through more accurate state tracking even when agents survive. However, among the 10 death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences. When both modalities survive, the gap is small but non-negligible ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 90\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-44.89$, $-33.37$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-13.66$, $-5.11$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-27.75$, $-17.09$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-10.65$, $-2.43$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-15.25$, $-6.38$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). 90\% confidence intervals computed via bootstrap. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.3\% of its decisions match the always-deterministic strategy.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates for four unconditional strategies. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle, which selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode, improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates that the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation---the routing signal is insufficient to improve upon simply always choosing the deterministic strategy. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, with a death rate (27.8\%) proportional to its 50/50 allocation.

Keyword-based routing (\S\ref{sec:rule}) fails structurally: match rates cluster at extremes (85.8\% or $<$5\%), precluding meaningful routing discrimination.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A natural question is whether the episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router. We investigate this as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal, testing two architectures.

\paragraph{Classification head.} We append a linear classification head to the LLM's hidden representations, trained on episode-level routing labels. Across all configurations tested---class weighting, focal loss, minority undersampling, and temperature scaling---the classifier degenerates to predicting always-deterministic (0\% internal recall in all cases). The episode-level labeling granularity does not transfer to step-level classification through this architecture.

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune a smaller model (Qwen3-1.7B) to internalize routing decisions within its generation process. Gradient accumulation is critical: with gradient accumulation of 1, the model fails to achieve meaningful routing discrimination (internal recall 26.7\%); with gradient accumulation of 8, the model becomes non-degenerate (internal recall above 50\% across all seeds). We report results for the non-degenerate configuration (gradient accumulation 8). At both the logit and generation levels, accuracy is 65.4\%, balanced accuracy 59.3\%, and internal recall 51.5\%---no logit-generation gap exists for this configuration, demonstrating a weak but above-chance routing signal beyond what a degenerate always-deterministic predictor captures.

\paragraph{Cross-seed stability.} Balanced accuracy is stable across seeds (57--59\%), but internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%---the signal exists but is insufficient for practical routing, consistent with the death avoidance decomposition: when ${\sim}90\%$ of routing value is death avoidance, the learnable non-death signal is structurally thin.

\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (90\% CI $[-15.25, -6.38]$) with 17.6\% death rate, allocating 97.0\% of episodes to deterministic computation. It did not significantly outperform always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-13.66, -5.11]$)---CIs overlap substantially. The few internally-routed episodes include cases where the agent dies, and misrouting cost exceeds the benefit of correctly routing rare internal-preferred episodes. This is a direct corollary of death avoidance dominance: any router that occasionally routes internally risks catastrophic failure, while always-deterministic avoids this risk entirely.

Three observations follow. First, logit-level success rules out uninformative labels, pointing to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the classification head bottleneck. Second, in death-dominated environments, always-deterministic is near-optimal and any learned router must overcome asymmetric misrouting cost (death vs.\ marginal score improvement). Third, when DAF $\approx 90\%$, router headroom is structurally limited to the ${\sim}10\%$ non-death signal.

\section{Conclusion}

We introduce episode-level counterfactual analysis for understanding routing between LLM internal reasoning and deterministic computation, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy (98.2\% tie rate) in sparse-reward settings. On 11 ScienceWorld task types (245 episodes), death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of routing signal ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60 \rightarrow \Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$; $r = 0.9698$, $p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). The value of deterministic computation is predominantly concentrated in preventing catastrophic failures.

\paragraph{Limitations and Future Work.} Our analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct). ScienceWorld's $-100$ death penalty may amplify the death avoidance signal; environments with less catastrophic failure modes could exhibit lower DAF values. Our counterfactual labels operate at episode granularity, limiting router training resolution. Cross-seed instability in internal recall (51.5--81.8\%) warrants further investigation. Step-level labeling with denser rewards (e.g., DAgger-style \cite{dagger-2011} imitation learning) could improve both decomposition and router training. Extension to code-interpreter routing and multi-LLM evaluation would test generalization beyond simulator-based environments.

